export const priorityArticles = {
  "hacoo-must-haves-nice-to-haves-deal-breakers": {
    ...common,
    title: "Hacoo Must-Haves, Nice-to-Haves and Deal-Breakers: A Clear Shortlist Method",
    excerpt: "Sort every comparison criterion by consequence before a favourite candidate quietly changes the rules.",
    keyword: "Hacoo comparison criteria",
    imageAlt: "Hacoo shortlist candidate used to separate must-haves from preferences and deal-breakers",
    imageCaption: "Three labels make a shortlist easier to defend: required, preferred and disqualifying.",
    sections: [
      ["Start with one job, not a general wish", `This method begins after you already have two or three Hacoo candidates. Write one sentence that describes the job the product must do, the person using it and the situation in which it matters. “A compact bag for a 28-centimetre tablet, a water bottle and a daily train commute” is useful. “A nice everyday bag” is not. The first statement creates observable consequences; the second invites the most attractive image to define the purchase. Hacoo publicly presents itself as a content-sharing and discovery community where creators and users provide inspiration and personal experience. That material can help you discover candidates, but it should not decide your requirement. Keep category, variant and intended use stable across the shortlist. If one candidate is a travel bag and another is a small evening bag, decide which job matters before comparing them. A shortlist is only fair when every candidate is being asked to solve the same problem.`],
      ["Define must-haves by the consequence of failure", `A must-have is not simply something you strongly like. It is a condition whose absence makes the candidate unable to perform the named job. For the commute bag, a closing method, minimum internal dimensions and a carry style you can use on a crowded train may be must-haves. For a layer worn over a sweatshirt, a usable chest width and the required closure may qualify. Write four to seven must-haves and add the consequence beside each one: “If the internal length is below 28 centimetres, the tablet does not fit.” This sentence stops preference from disguising itself as necessity. Use measurements, named functions, bundle contents or exact compatibility rather than vague words such as premium, comfortable or high quality. If you cannot explain what fails when a condition is absent, move it to nice-to-have until stronger reasoning shows otherwise.`],
      ["Keep nice-to-haves visible but deliberately weaker", `Nice-to-haves still belong in the comparison; they simply come after functional eligibility. A preferred colour, decorative finish, extra pocket or lower current item price can distinguish two candidates that both pass the required conditions. Record these preferences in a separate block and rank them rather than giving everything the same status. This prevents an eight-point visual preference from compensating for a missing dimension that determines fit. It also makes a trade-off readable: Candidate A meets every requirement and has the preferred colour, while Candidate B meets every requirement and provides clearer care information. You can choose between those advantages without pretending either one proves quality. Avoid adding points for labels such as viral, bestselling or creator favourite unless the popularity itself is relevant to your use, which is unusual. Attention is not evidence that the product will perform your specific job.`],
      ["Write deal-breakers before looking at totals", `A deal-breaker is a condition that ends consideration immediately. It may be the clear failure of a must-have, a hard budget ceiling for the exact option, an incompatible device model, a bundle that omits a required part or an unresolved fact with consequences too large to accept. Write deal-breakers before scoring so that a favourite cannot receive an exception later. Use three outcomes for each candidate: pass, fail or unknown. Unknown is not half a pass. If the listing never connects a measurement to the selected size, the fit criterion remains unknown even when the photographs look persuasive. Decide in advance which unknowns pause the decision. A missing decorative detail may be tolerable; an unknown internal dimension for the object the bag must carry may stop the candidate. Applying the same rule to every option is more important than choosing a sophisticated scoring system.`],
      ["Attach evidence to the exact variant", `Each criterion needs a short evidence note tied to the exact colour, size, bundle or version under review. Copy the visible option label, record the date and note whether selecting it changes the image, specification, contents or price. A general size chart elsewhere on the page may not clearly apply to every variant. A photograph can show accessories that are not included in the selected bundle. A starting price can belong to a smaller or different option. Classify evidence as direct, indirect or absent. Direct evidence answers the criterion for the selected option. Indirect evidence leaves an assumption. Absent evidence provides no answer. This classification is not a product-quality rating and does not prove authenticity, durability or comfort. It simply prevents the comparison from turning a creator caption, a promotional image or a nearby option into a fact about the candidate you might choose.`],
      ["Use a two-stage table instead of one blended score", `Build the table in two stages. Stage one contains must-haves and deal-breakers with pass, fail and unknown. Eliminate a candidate that clearly fails a genuine requirement; pause one whose high-consequence fact is unknown. Stage two contains only the eligible candidates and compares nice-to-haves. You may rank those preferences or use a small weighted score, but keep the result subordinate to stage one. This structure prevents the classic spreadsheet error in which ten cosmetic points outweigh one functional failure. It also allows every candidate to be rejected, which is a healthy result when the shortlist is weak. Do not lower the requirement merely because no product passes. Return to discovery, seek clearer evidence or decide that the purchase should wait. A good comparison protects the job you meant to solve instead of producing a winner at any cost.`],
      ["Test the classification with a reversal question", `Before finalising, challenge every label. Ask: “If this condition disappeared, would the product still perform the stated job?” If yes, it is probably a nice-to-have. Ask: “If this fact remained unknown after payment, could the result become unusable or meaningfully costly?” If yes, it may be a pause condition or deal-breaker. Then reverse the candidates. Would you apply the same rule to the option you like less? Unequal enforcement is a strong sign that preference has entered the evidence stage. This review is especially useful when one candidate has better photography or more social attention. Visual polish can make ordinary preferences feel essential. The reversal question restores symmetry without pretending personal taste is irrelevant. Taste is allowed; it simply belongs after the candidate has proved that it can meet the job.`],
      ["Finish with a decision sentence and a stop rule", `Write one final sentence naming the job, the passing candidate and the evidence for each must-have. For example: “Candidate B remains because the selected option lists the required closure, its recorded internal length fits the tablet and the current bundle includes the strap needed for the commute.” Add the preferred feature that breaks the tie, if any. If the sentence needs an unsupported claim about material performance, comfort, authenticity, quality, future availability or delivery, the decision is not ready. State the unresolved fact instead. Your stop rule might be: choose only when every must-have passes, no deal-breaker is present and every high-consequence unknown has been resolved. Otherwise recheck or choose none. Reopen the exact option before acting because live pages, variants and prices can change. The method does not guarantee an outcome; it ensures the final choice still answers the original requirement.`],
    ],
  },
  "limit-hacoo-shortlist-to-three-candidates": {
    ...common,
    title: "How to Limit a Hacoo Shortlist to Three Serious Candidates",
    excerpt: "Reduce choice overload with a transparent eligibility pass before detailed product comparison begins.",
    keyword: "Hacoo shortlist",
    imageAlt: "Three Hacoo product candidates arranged as a focused shortlist",
    imageCaption: "Three serious candidates create enough contrast without turning the decision into endless browsing.",
    sections: [
      ["Why three is a useful working limit", `A large collection can be useful during discovery, but it is a poor decision surface. When eight or twelve Hacoo candidates remain open, people tend to compare different attributes on different pages and remember only the most striking images. Limiting the working shortlist to three creates enough variation to reveal trade-offs while keeping the evidence visible. This is not a claim that three is universally optimal. It is an operating rule: one baseline candidate, one credible alternative and one option that tests a meaningful trade-off. Hacoo's public positioning emphasises discovery, creator content and personal experience, so the platform can naturally produce many possibilities. The decision stage needs a different behaviour. Freeze discovery temporarily, define the job and reduce the field before reading more promotional material. If a fourth candidate appears later, it must replace an existing one by passing the same eligibility rules rather than simply expanding the list.`],
      ["Create an eligibility filter before choosing favourites", `Write five fast eligibility questions that every candidate must answer. Does it serve the same intended use? Is the exact variant identifiable? Is there enough information to test the most important measurement, capacity or compatibility condition? Is the current option within the item-price boundary you set? Is the page available in your actual region at the time of checking? These questions are not a quality score. They only establish whether detailed comparison is possible. Mark each candidate eligible, ineligible or pending evidence. Remove obvious mismatches immediately: a bag that cannot be connected to the required device size, a clothing option without any way to assess the critical measurement or a bundle that omits the necessary component. A beautiful candidate that cannot yet answer the job is not a serious finalist. It can remain in a parking list while you seek evidence, but it should not occupy one of the three decision slots.`],
      ["Choose candidates that expose real trade-offs", `Do not fill the shortlist with three nearly identical pages unless the decision truly concerns a small variant difference. Select candidates that reveal the main alternatives inside the brief. A jacket shortlist might include the clearest fit evidence, the simplest care requirement and the lowest current item price among options that pass eligibility. A bag shortlist might compare maximum usable capacity, lower carry bulk and better closure information. Describe the trade-off in one line per candidate. If you cannot explain why an option is present, it may be there because of novelty rather than decision value. The goal is not to represent every style. It is to create a compact set in which each candidate has a plausible route to winning under the same job. This makes later weights meaningful and stops the comparison from rewarding duplicate strengths three times.`],
      ["Use a replacement rule to stop shortlist creep", `Choice overload often returns when a new link appears after the shortlist is built. Use a replacement rule: a new candidate enters only if it clearly improves one important criterion without failing an existing must-have, or if it resolves a high-consequence unknown present in the current three. Compare it first against the weakest current finalist, not against an imaginary perfect product. If it does not replace that candidate, park it and continue. Record the reason for replacement—clearer exact measurements, compatible model information, included part, simpler care evidence or a meaningful price difference for the same type of option. “Looks better” is allowed as a preference, but it should not reopen discovery unless appearance has defined importance. This rule protects the time already spent and makes the process auditable. It also prevents endless scrolling from being mistaken for research.`],
      ["Standardise the evidence card", `Give every finalist the same compact evidence card: exact URL, date checked, exact option label, first-image match, current item price, required measurements or specifications, bundle contents, three strongest facts and three unknowns. Do not copy whole pages. The card should show what the decision depends on. If one candidate has ten fields and another has only two, the difference itself matters: the second candidate carries more uncertainty. Keep the source of each fact visible. A creator caption, customer review, image and listing specification do not have the same evidentiary role. A review may reveal a question; it does not automatically supply a measurement for your option. By standardising the card before scoring, you reduce memory bias and make it easier to remove a candidate whose apparent strength came from better presentation rather than better support for the use case.`],
      ["Run one fast knockout round", `Before detailed weights, test the three cards against the non-negotiable conditions. Use pass, unknown and fail. Remove a clear fail. Pause a high-consequence unknown when the purchase would be unusable if the assumption is wrong. Do not allow a strong colour, price or popularity signal to cancel a failure. If only one candidate remains, you still do not have an automatic winner; you have one candidate worth a final check. If none remain, the shortlist has done its job by preventing a forced purchase. Return to discovery with a sharper brief that names the missing evidence or failed condition. If all three remain, proceed to weighted criteria or a simpler ordered comparison. This knockout round is deliberately fast because its purpose is to save the deeper analysis for options that can plausibly succeed.`],
      ["Compare in one sitting, then take a cooling-off break", `Once the cards are complete, compare them in one sitting so that the evidence is evaluated under the same standard. Write the provisional result and the unresolved fact that could change it. Then stop browsing for a defined period—several hours or a day, depending on urgency. The break exposes whether the leading option solves the job or merely held your attention. When you return, read the original use sentence before looking at images. If the favourite changes only because the visual excitement faded, the evidence was probably weak. If the same candidate still passes every requirement and retains the clearest trade-off, the decision is more stable. A cooling-off period is especially valuable when social posts or scarcity language create pressure. It costs nothing and keeps discovery energy separate from purchase reasoning.`],
      ["Archive the decision, not every rejected link", `Finish with a one-paragraph record: the intended use, the three candidates, the knockout result, the decisive criterion, unresolved uncertainty and final outcome—choose, recheck or none. Save only enough evidence to reproduce the reasoning. There is no need to maintain a permanent database of every rejected link. The shortlist can expire because options, availability and prices change. If you revisit it later, recheck each exact variant rather than assuming the earlier card remains current. This keeps the method lightweight and honest. The value of a three-candidate shortlist is not the number itself; it is the discipline of making every place earn its role. The process narrows attention, applies the same test to each option and leaves room for the conclusion that no candidate currently meets the requirement.`],
    ],
  },
  "hacoo-knockout-rules-before-weighted-scores": {
    ...common,
    title: "Hacoo Knockout Rules: Eliminate Weak Candidates Before Weighted Scores",
    excerpt: "Protect non-negotiable requirements from being averaged away by attractive but secondary features.",
    keyword: "Hacoo knockout rules",
    imageAlt: "Hacoo comparison candidate assessed with knockout rules before scoring",
    imageCaption: "A knockout gate asks whether the candidate can do the job before asking how much you like it.",
    sections: [
      ["Separate eligibility from preference", `Weighted scores are useful only after every candidate has shown that it can plausibly perform the intended job. Otherwise a product can collect points for colour, photography, price or extra features while failing the one condition that makes it usable. Begin with an eligibility layer called the knockout gate. Write the use case, then list the small number of conditions that cannot be traded away. A phone case must match the exact model and opening layout. A bag must fit the required object and close in the way the use demands. A garment intended for layering must have enough measurement evidence to test that layer. These are not claims about general quality. They are conditions created by your brief. Hacoo's discovery environment can surface persuasive images and creator commentary; the gate makes sure inspiration does not become a substitute for compatibility.`],
      ["Design rules that can produce a clear result", `A knockout rule needs an observable condition and a declared outcome. “Must be good quality” cannot be tested from a listing. “Selected bundle must explicitly include the detachable strap” can. “Must fit well” is vague; “listed chest width must exceed the measured reference garment by the allowance I require” is actionable. For every rule, write what counts as pass, fail and unknown. Pass means direct evidence supports the exact option. Fail means direct evidence contradicts the requirement. Unknown means the information is absent, ambiguous or not connected to the variant. Decide whether an unknown pauses or removes the candidate according to consequence. Do not classify all missing details as failures, but do not reward them with assumed passes. A rule is useful when another careful reader could apply it to the same evidence and understand the result.`],
      ["Keep the gate short and high consequence", `Most comparisons need three to six knockout rules. More than that often means preferences have leaked into the gate. Rank possible rules by consequence: would failure make the item unusable, incompatible, outside a hard limit or impossible to evaluate responsibly? Include only those with a meaningful yes. Colour can be a gate when the item is required for a uniform; otherwise it may be a preference. Price can be a gate when the exact current option exceeds a fixed item budget, but do not confuse a displayed base price with the cost of the selected variant. A delivery deadline may be a use-case gate only when supported by current information and a realistic buffer; it should not become a universal promise. The shorter list protects its authority. When every small desire becomes a knockout, the method rejects useful candidates for cosmetic reasons.`],
      ["Apply the rules in the same order", `Check identity first: correct destination, product type and exact option. Then check compatibility and dimensions, bundle contents, hard price boundary and any other use-specific condition. The fixed order prevents you from spending time on a candidate that already failed a basic identity test. Use a table with candidates as columns and rules as rows. Add one evidence note and date to every cell. Do not copy a fact from one colour or size to another unless the page clearly connects them. Do not use a visually similar product to fill the gap. If a page changes during the comparison, restart the affected column. Symmetry matters: the candidate you prefer should not receive a looser interpretation of “unknown” than the candidate you like less. Read the evidence notes without images once to test whether the results still feel consistent.`],
      ["Do not let weighted points resurrect a failure", `After the gate, remove any clear fail from the scored table. A candidate that lacks a required component cannot return because it earns strong marks for colour and price. If a high-consequence unknown is unresolved, keep the candidate in a separate hold state rather than assigning it average points. This is the central safeguard. A weighted score answers “Which eligible candidate better fits my preferences?” It does not answer “Can this ineligible candidate compensate for incompatibility?” Use zero only when the evidence supports a zero result, not as a substitute for missing information. If the remaining shortlist has one candidate, conduct a final evidence check rather than celebrating an automatic winner. If it has none, preserve the brief and seek a better option. Forced winners are a sign that the gate has been ignored.`],
      ["Handle unknowns with consequence and reversibility", `Two unknowns can deserve different treatment. An unknown decorative finish is usually reversible in the sense that disappointment does not prevent basic use. An unknown device model, garment measurement or included essential part can make the purchase unusable. Rate each unknown by consequence and by the ease of resolving it before acting. Ask whether another current image, specification or clarification could answer the question. If yes, state exactly what evidence is needed. If not, decide whether your tolerance permits the uncertainty. Do not replace the fact with a creator claim or a review of a different variant. The outcome can be “hold,” which is different from fail. Hold preserves a promising candidate without allowing it to enter the weighted comparison. This keeps uncertainty visible and avoids pretending that missing information is negative evidence.`],
      ["Audit the gate for hidden bias", `Before accepting the survivors, perform three audits. First, the favourite test: would the same evidence pass if it belonged to the least attractive candidate? Second, the consequence test: does every knockout condition genuinely stop the named use? Third, the evidence test: is each pass tied to the exact option rather than an adjacent image or general statement? Remove or downgrade rules that fail these audits. Also look for duplicated gates, such as using both “fits laptop” and “minimum internal length” when they express the same requirement. Duplicate rules can make one issue appear more important than intended. The audit takes minutes and prevents false objectivity. A table can look rigorous while encoding a preferred outcome; the questions expose that problem before numbers create extra confidence.`],
      ["Record the result as a decision checkpoint", `Write the gate result before moving to weights: which candidates passed, failed or were held, and why. Name the exact evidence that would release a held option. Then freeze the gate. If you later change a knockout rule, record the reason and apply it to every candidate, including those already removed. This prevents quiet rule changes designed to rescue a favourite. Proceed to weighted scoring only with the survivors, and keep the gate table beside the score. The final conclusion should mention both: “Candidate A passed all four use-case gates and led on the two highest-weight preferences.” Reopen the exact option immediately before acting. The checkpoint is not a guarantee of product quality, authenticity, availability or delivery. It is evidence that the candidate still satisfies the conditions you declared non-negotiable.`],
    ],
  },
  "weight-hacoo-comparison-criteria": {
    ...common,
    title: "How to Weight Hacoo Comparison Criteria Without False Precision",
    excerpt: "Use consequences, ranges and sensitivity checks instead of decorative decimal scores.",
    keyword: "weight Hacoo comparison criteria",
    imageAlt: "Hacoo candidates compared with transparent criterion weights",
    imageCaption: "Weights should express consequence, not manufacture mathematical certainty.",
    sections: [
      ["Use weights only after eligibility", `Weights cannot repair a failed requirement. Start with a shortlist whose exact variants have passed your non-negotiable gates or have only low-consequence unknowns that you deliberately accept. Then use weights to describe trade-offs among eligible candidates. State the job at the top of the table. A commuter bag, occasional event layer and daily walking shoe need different criteria even when the product images look similar. Hacoo's public discovery and creator environment can provide many attributes, but the amount of content around an item should not determine its weight. Weight is your statement about consequence: how much would this factor change whether the product performs the job and gets used? This distinction stops a visually prominent feature from dominating simply because it is easier to see or more frequently discussed.`],
      ["Choose four to six independent criteria", `A short set is easier to explain and less likely to count the same idea twice. For clothing, possible criteria include fit-evidence strength, compatibility with the intended layer, care burden, material-information completeness and current item-price fit. For a bag, capacity evidence, closure, carry method, empty dimensions and price may be more relevant. Test independence by asking whether two criteria would rise and fall for the same reason. “Fits the laptop” and “adequate internal width” may be duplicates. Combine them or define different roles. Avoid universal categories such as quality, value or best. They hide multiple judgements and encourage unsupported claims. Each criterion should have a one-sentence scoring rule that identifies what a low, middle and high score means using evidence available for the selected option.`],
      ["Assign weights from consequences", `Imagine the top criterion failed while the others were strong. How much would that change the decision? Use that answer to rank the criteria before assigning numbers. Give the highest-consequence factor the largest share, then distribute the remainder so all weights total 100. Round numbers such as 30, 25, 20, 15 and 10 are usually enough. Decimal weights imply a precision the reasoning does not have. Write one justification beside each weight: “Fit evidence receives 30 because unusable dimensions defeat the layering brief.” If you cannot explain the difference between 20 and 25, use the same weight or simplify. Set weights before scoring candidates. Adjust them only when the use case changes, and then rescore every option. Changing a weight after seeing the total is a common way to make a favourite appear objectively superior.`],
      ["Define an evidence-based scale", `A five-point scale works when every level has a meaning. Zero can mean direct evidence contradicts the criterion; one weak support; three adequate support with a remaining limitation; five strong direct support for the exact option. Unknown should stay unknown rather than becoming 2.5. Add a separate confidence label—high, medium or low—to show how well the score is supported. A precise measurement linked to the variant can create higher confidence than a general size word. Several current consistent images may support visible construction better than one cropped post. The confidence label does not rate product quality. It rates your knowledge. This separation prevents a polished page from receiving an inflated performance score and allows two candidates with similar totals to be distinguished by the strength of evidence behind them.`],
      ["Calculate a range when important facts are missing", `For each known score, multiply the criterion weight by the score and divide by five. For a 30-point criterion, a score of four contributes 24. When an important factor is unknown, calculate a lower and upper bound instead of choosing an invented middle. The lower bound uses the lowest defensible result; the upper bound uses the highest result the available evidence could reasonably support. If Candidate A spans 62–82 and Candidate B sits at 74 with stronger confidence, the ranking is unresolved. The missing fact could change the winner. If A spans 76–82 and B reaches 68, the uncertainty may not alter the conclusion. Ranges turn missing information into a practical research priority. Resolve the unknown with the largest ability to change the result rather than collecting more facts that have no decision impact.`],
      ["Run a sensitivity check", `Change the two largest weights by five or ten points while keeping the total at 100. Does the winner change? If a tiny adjustment reverses the result, the score does not support a confident ranking. Treat the candidates as effectively tied and use stronger evidence, lower downside or the original brief as the tie-breaker. If the result remains stable across reasonable weights, the decision is less dependent on an arbitrary number. Also remove one low-weight preference entirely and recalculate. A candidate should not win solely because several decorative details were counted separately. Sensitivity checking is more honest than adding decimals. It shows where judgement matters and prevents the final score from looking like an external measurement. The numbers organise your reasoning; they do not discover an objective best product.`],
      ["Read totals beside evidence and downside", `Never publish or save a total without the gate result, confidence labels and key unknowns. A score of 78 supported by exact measurements and clear option contents is different from 82 built on indirect images and a general description. Compare the downside if the most important assumption is wrong. The candidate with a slightly lower total may be the safer decision when its evidence is clearer and the consequences of error are smaller. Use the total to focus discussion, not end it. Write a short explanation: which two criteria created the lead, which unknown could still matter and why the weighting fits the intended use. This narrative protects the table from being copied into a context with different needs. Another buyer may rationally choose different weights because the job is different.`],
      ["Finish with a rounded decision rule", `Choose a simple rule before accepting the result: all gates pass, no high-consequence factor remains low confidence, and the leading candidate stays ahead under a reasonable sensitivity check. If the rule fails, recheck the named fact or choose none. Do not convert a score such as 76.4 into a claim that one product is 2.7 percent better. Round to whole numbers and describe the difference qualitatively—clear lead, narrow lead or unresolved tie. Reopen the live variant before acting and update any changed input. The method cannot guarantee performance, quality, authenticity, availability or delivery. It can show that the selected candidate better fits your declared criteria under the evidence available on the checking date. That is the appropriate level of confidence for a practical Hacoo comparison.`],
    ],
  },
  "compare-incomplete-hacoo-product-pages": {
    ...common,
    title: "How to Compare Incomplete Hacoo Product Pages Without Filling the Gaps",
    excerpt: "Keep missing facts visible, compare only supported fields and decide when uncertainty should pause the shortlist.",
    keyword: "compare incomplete Hacoo product pages",
    imageAlt: "Hacoo product candidates with complete and missing comparison fields",
    imageCaption: "A blank field is evidence about uncertainty, not permission to copy an answer from a similar listing.",
    sections: [
      ["Treat incompleteness as part of the decision", `Two Hacoo pages rarely provide information in exactly the same form. One may show clear dimensions but little material information; another may provide several images but no option-linked measurements. The wrong response is to make the table look complete by borrowing assumptions from a similar product. Keep the missing field visible. Hacoo publicly describes an open content-sharing and discovery community that includes creator and user contributions. Those contributions can add context, but they are not automatically specifications for the exact product variant you are comparing. Begin with the intended use and identify the five or six facts that could change the decision. Then distinguish missing evidence from negative evidence. A blank measurement does not prove the product is too small; it proves that you cannot yet test fit. This preserves accuracy and shows which uncertainty deserves attention.`],
      ["Build an evidence map before scoring", `Create a row for every decision-relevant field and classify the evidence for each candidate as direct, indirect, conflicting or absent. Direct means the page connects the information to the selected option. Indirect means a nearby image, general description or review suggests an answer without establishing it. Conflicting means two current elements disagree. Absent means no usable answer appears. Add the date and location of the evidence—option selector, size table, specification, image or review. Do not score yet. The map reveals whether the comparison is symmetrical. Candidate A may look more complete simply because its page has longer marketing copy, while Candidate B may provide the one exact dimension that matters. Evidence quantity is not evidence relevance. The map turns a vague feeling that a listing is incomplete into a concrete set of knowns and unknowns.`],
      ["Compare only on common supported ground", `First compare the fields for which both candidates have evidence at a similar level. If both list exact outer dimensions, that field is comparable. If one has a material percentage and the other only says “soft,” material is not a fair scored comparison. You can state that A provides clearer material information, but you cannot conclude that A's material performs better. When evidence types differ, compare information completeness as a separate criterion rather than converting a description into a product-performance score. This keeps the analysis useful without rewarding silence or marketing language. It also protects the weaker page from invented negatives. The question is not “Which page tells the better story?” but “Which candidate has enough supported information to decide the job, and where could missing evidence still reverse the result?”`],
      ["Prioritise gaps by consequence", `Not every blank deserves more research. Rank gaps by the harm if the optimistic assumption is wrong and by their ability to change the winner. Missing decorative detail may have low consequence. Missing device compatibility, required measurement, bundle component or hard care restriction can have high consequence. Use a simple matrix: high or low consequence, easy or difficult to resolve. Resolve high-consequence, easy gaps first. Place high-consequence, difficult gaps into a hold decision. Accept low-consequence gaps only when your tolerance is explicit. This prevents the search for completeness from becoming endless. It also prevents a candidate from advancing because its most dangerous uncertainty was ignored while many minor facts were collected. A useful comparison is not the one with the most filled cells; it is the one that makes the consequential blanks impossible to miss.`],
      ["Do not transfer facts across variants", `A colour, size or bundle can change measurements, included parts, price and images. Record the exact option label and verify that every fact belongs to it. A size table may apply broadly, but do not assume that a photographed accessory is included in every bundle. A review may describe a different colour or production version. A creator may link to a page after the listing changes. Similarity does not create identity. If the relationship is unclear, keep the evidence indirect. The same rule applies across separate pages: do not borrow the material, capacity or care information from a visually similar item. This restraint may leave the table incomplete, but it prevents the more serious error of building a confident conclusion from mismatched evidence. A shorter honest comparison is more useful than a complete-looking one based on transfers.`],
      ["Use ranges and scenarios instead of invented midpoint scores", `When a missing fact can vary within a defensible range, calculate scenarios. Suppose Candidate A has an unknown capacity criterion worth 25 points. Show the total if that criterion fails and if it receives the highest score current evidence could support. If A still leads in the lower scenario, the missing fact may not block the decision. If the result flips, the comparison remains open. Do not assign an average merely because the table expects a number. For nonnumeric facts, write conditional statements: “A is eligible only if the selected bundle includes the strap,” or “B becomes preferable if the listed width applies to the chosen size.” Conditions turn uncertainty into a testable next step and show exactly why the decision is paused. They are more informative than a false single score.`],
      ["Know when to stop seeking evidence", `Set a stopping rule before opening more links. Stop when every must-have has direct evidence, or when an unresolved high-consequence fact cannot be verified and you are unwilling to accept the risk. Also stop when new information no longer changes eligibility, ranking or confidence. More browsing is not automatically better research. It can introduce adjacent products, old reviews and conflicting variants that make the record less coherent. Keep a named request list—one measurement, one bundle confirmation, one compatibility statement—rather than a general desire to “research more.” If the answer never arrives, choose a candidate with adequate evidence or choose none. The page does not owe the table completeness, and the table should not manufacture it.`],
      ["Write the conclusion around what is known", `A responsible conclusion names the supported comparison and the remaining limits. For example: “Candidate B currently fits the brief more clearly because its exact option provides the required dimensions and closure information. Candidate A remains on hold because the internal length is not linked to the selected version.” Avoid turning that sentence into a general quality ranking. Reopen the pages before acting, verify that the evidence still belongs to the same options and record any change. If a missing fact has become available, update the relevant row rather than rewriting the whole analysis. The result may be choose B, request one fact, wait or choose none. Each is more useful than a polished winner built by filling gaps with assumptions.`],
    ],
  },
  "hacoo-comparison-confidence-labels": {
    ...common,
    title: "Hacoo Comparison Confidence Labels: Show Evidence Strength Without Rating Quality",
    excerpt: "Add high, medium and low confidence to each comparison claim while keeping product quality claims separate.",
    keyword: "Hacoo comparison confidence labels",
    imageAlt: "Hacoo shortlist evidence marked with high medium and low confidence labels",
    imageCaption: "Confidence describes the support behind a claim; it does not declare the product good or bad.",
    sections: [
      ["Define confidence narrowly", `A confidence label should answer one question: how strongly does the available evidence support this specific comparison claim for the selected option on the checking date? It should not rate overall product quality, seller reliability, authenticity or likely satisfaction. A listing can provide high-confidence dimensions and still leave comfort unknown. A review can offer medium-confidence evidence about a visible detail without predicting another buyer's result. Hacoo's public identity as a community and discovery platform means that official pages, creator content, customer reports and listing details can appear together. They have different roles. Confidence labels help you preserve those roles instead of blending every statement into one impression. Write the claim first—“the selected bag is listed with a 30-centimetre outer length”—then label the evidence, not the product.`],
      ["Create a three-level rubric", `Use high, medium and low only when the rules are clear. High confidence can require direct, current evidence linked to the exact variant, with no visible contradiction. Medium can cover evidence that is relevant but indirect, such as a general specification likely to apply across options or several consistent images without a written measurement. Low can describe a creator caption, isolated review, old screenshot, cropped image or statement whose variant is unclear. Absent is better than low when there is no usable evidence at all. Write the rubric beside the table so labels do not shift between candidates. Do not use numerical percentages; there is no dataset supporting “85 percent confidence.” Three levels are enough to show whether the conclusion rests on exact facts, reasonable but incomplete context or fragile inference.`],
      ["Label each criterion, not the whole product", `Overall confidence hides the shape of uncertainty. Apply the label to each criterion: fit evidence high, material information low, bundle contents medium, current price high. This allows a candidate to be strong where it matters and weak elsewhere. It also makes the next research step obvious. If the highest-weight criterion has low confidence, the decision may pause even when the total looks good. If only a decorative preference is low confidence, you may accept the uncertainty. Keep performance and confidence in separate columns. Performance asks how well the evidence meets the criterion; confidence asks how much you trust that interpretation. A candidate can have a favourable 4/5 result with low confidence, which should be treated differently from a 4/5 supported by exact option-linked information.`],
      ["Use source type and freshness", `Record whether evidence comes from the current option interface, a specification, image, customer report, creator post or official platform page. Then record the date. Source type alone does not determine truth, but it affects what the evidence can support. An official platform page can explain platform policy but usually cannot prove the measurements of an independent listing. A current listing specification can support a stated dimension but not real-world durability. A detailed review can identify a question about one received item but not establish a universal batch outcome. Freshness matters because links, options and content can change. When a source is old or its product identity is uncertain, lower confidence or mark the claim unresolved. This source discipline is more valuable than a decorative verification badge.`],
      ["Reduce confidence when evidence conflicts", `Conflict is not a reason to choose the most convenient version. If an image label, text specification and option name disagree, record the disagreement and lower confidence. Identify which evidence is closest to the selected variant and what clarification would resolve the conflict. Do not average two incompatible dimensions or silently prefer the larger value. If the conflict affects a must-have, place the candidate on hold. For a low-consequence preference, keep the uncertainty visible and continue if your tolerance allows it. Also check whether translation, unit conversion or image perspective explains the difference before treating it as a product change. The goal is not to solve every conflict; it is to prevent a confident score from hiding one. A clear conflict note can be more useful than a forced answer.`],
      ["Prevent confidence from becoming a quality score", `Labels can be misused when “high confidence” starts to mean “high quality.” Keep the wording attached to the claim: high confidence in the listed measurement, medium confidence in bundle contents, low confidence in colour appearance. Never write “high-confidence product” or rank candidates by the average of all confidence labels. Evidence completeness is valuable because it supports a decision, but it does not prove construction, durability, comfort or authenticity. A product page with clearer information may be easier to evaluate without being objectively better. State that distinction in the comparison summary. It allows you to prefer the clearer candidate for a high-consequence use while remaining honest about what has and has not been tested.`],
      ["Use confidence to direct the next check", `When two candidates are close, identify the low-confidence claim with the largest decision weight. Seek evidence for that claim first. If Candidate A's fit evidence is low and worth 30 points while Candidate B's colour evidence is low and worth five, the fit question has greater power to change the result. Write the exact evidence request: measurement tied to the selected size, a clear list of bundle contents or a current image showing the required closure. Stop when the new evidence no longer changes eligibility or ranking. This turns confidence labels into a research queue rather than a warning decoration. It also prevents time being spent collecting additional facts about the candidate already favoured while ignoring the uncertainty that could overturn the decision.`],
      ["Publish a conclusion with calibrated language", `Use language that matches the label. High confidence supports “the selected option lists.” Medium supports “the available page evidence suggests, but does not clearly link.” Low supports “a limited source indicates, so this remains unresolved.” Finish with the chosen outcome and the fact that could change it. Recheck the exact option immediately before acting and update any label affected by a new image, price, specification or variant. Confidence is time-bound. The method cannot certify product quality or guarantee an outcome. It can make the comparison more transparent by showing where the reasoning is supported, where it is provisional and why a no-buy or recheck result may be more appropriate than a forced winner.`],
    ],
  },
  "compare-specific-hacoo-variants": {
    ...common,
    title: "Compare Specific Hacoo Variants Instead of Averaging a Whole Listing",
    excerpt: "Keep colour, size, bundle, image, specification and price attached to the exact option under review.",
    keyword: "compare Hacoo variants",
    imageAlt: "Exact Hacoo product variants compared as separate candidates",
    imageCaption: "The listing is a container; the selected size, colour and bundle are the actual comparison candidate.",
    sections: [
      ["Make the variant the unit of comparison", `A Hacoo listing may contain several colours, sizes, bundles or versions that differ in image, specification, included parts and price. Treating the whole page as one product creates an average that nobody can actually select. Write the exact option label at the top of every candidate card. If the decision concerns size, record size and colour; if it concerns a set, record the bundle name and quantity; if it concerns compatibility, record the precise model. Hacoo's discovery environment can lead through images or creator links, but the final comparison must return to the selectable option. A link is only a route to the listing. The candidate is the variant you could actually choose. This shift removes many false comparisons and makes evidence easier to attach.`],
      ["Capture a variant snapshot", `Record the page URL, date, option label, first image after selection, current listed price, visible specification and included contents. Note which elements change when you switch options. Some interfaces change the image but leave a general description; others change price or availability. Do not assume that a static table applies to every version when the page does not say so. Use a screenshot or concise notes for the facts that drive the decision, not for every visual detail. The snapshot provides a stable reference if the listing later changes and lets you compare candidates at the same identity level. It does not freeze inventory or guarantee a future price. Recheck before acting and update only the changed fields.`],
      ["Match each fact to the selected option", `For every measurement, material statement, accessory or compatibility claim, ask whether the page links it to the exact option. A general photograph of a bag with a strap does not prove the selected bundle includes the strap. A size chart can be useful, but the chosen size must be present and the measurement method understandable. A review may discuss a different colour or version. Keep these sources as context rather than direct proof. Use direct, indirect, conflicting or absent labels. This discipline prevents a visually rich listing from appearing more complete than it is and stops evidence from a premium bundle being transferred to a basic one. When the relationship is unclear, write the question rather than the answer you hope is true.`],
      ["Compare like with like", `Choose an equivalence rule before evaluating price or features. Compare single item with single item, same function with same function and options that meet the same hard requirements. If Candidate A's displayed price covers one piece while Candidate B's covers a two-piece set, the numbers are not directly comparable. If one shoe option is a different version, confirm that the relevant size and construction information still match. A lower starting price is not a saving when it belongs to a smaller bundle or incompatible option. State any unavoidable difference and explain how it affects the decision. The goal is not to erase every distinction; it is to prevent the option selector from quietly changing the object being scored.`],
      ["Handle colour and size as evidence variables", `Colour can affect visible detail, screen appearance and sometimes availability. Size affects fit evidence and may affect price or option status. Do not assume that photographs show colour under neutral light, and do not convert letter sizes through a universal table when garment measurements are available. Compare a reference garment measured with the same method. Record whether the listed dimension describes the body or the garment and whether tolerance is mentioned. If a size lacks a linked measurement, mark fit confidence lower. These steps do not predict comfort or real-world quality. They establish whether the selected variant can be compared to the requirement without borrowing an answer from another size or colour.`],
      ["Score variants separately", `A listing with three relevant variants creates three possible candidates, not one average candidate. Apply the same gates and criteria to each variant you seriously consider. One may pass the measurement gate but exceed the item-price boundary; another may fit the budget but lack required capacity. Do not combine their strengths into a fictional ideal. If two variants from the same page remain, keep separate columns. This can reveal that the actual contest is between options within one listing rather than between different pages. It also prevents a page-level review count or attractive hero image from overpowering the evidence attached to the variant. The score should describe what you could select now, under the current page state.`],
      ["Recheck identity after following a shared link", `Shared links can continue to load after images, titles or available options change. Run the identity sequence: product type, first image, title, option and region. If the destination no longer matches the card or note that brought you there, rebuild the variant snapshot. Do not preserve old evidence merely because the URL is unchanged. When a creator or spreadsheet mentions an option that is no longer selectable, the candidate is unavailable for the present comparison. Mark it as changed or removed rather than substituting the nearest visible version. This protects the record from silent listing drift and makes the checked date meaningful.`],
      ["Conclude with an option-level sentence", `The final sentence should name the exact variant: “The black large option remains because its linked measurements pass the layering requirement, the selected bundle lists the required piece and its current item price stays inside the stated limit.” If you can only say “this listing looks best,” the comparison has not reached option level. Add the decisive evidence and any remaining uncertainty. Immediately before acting, reopen the selector and confirm that the label, image, specification, contents and price still match the record. This process cannot guarantee stock, delivery, quality or authenticity. It can prevent the common mistake of selecting one option after comparing evidence from several different ones.`],
    ],
  },
  "hacoo-facts-vs-preference-scorecard": {
    ...common,
    title: "Hacoo Scorecard: Separate Listed Facts From Personal Preference",
    excerpt: "Build a comparison that keeps evidence, interpretation and taste in separate columns.",
    keyword: "Hacoo comparison scorecard",
    imageAlt: "Hacoo candidates assessed in a facts and preference scorecard",
    imageCaption: "Facts describe the page, interpretation tests the brief and preference records personal taste.",
    sections: [
      ["Use three layers instead of one opinion", `A useful Hacoo scorecard separates listed fact, decision interpretation and personal preference. The fact column records what the current page or option shows: measurement, closure, material statement, bundle contents or price. The interpretation column explains whether that fact meets the intended use. The preference column records taste: colour, shape, visual detail or brand-independent style appeal. Hacoo publicly describes a community where creators and users share content and experience, so persuasive opinions can appear beside product information. The scorecard does not reject those opinions; it prevents them from being mistaken for specifications. Start after discovery with two or three candidates serving the same job. A clean separation makes the conclusion easier to revise when a fact changes and easier to explain when two people reasonably prefer different eligible options.`],
      ["Write facts in neutral language", `Copy only the decision-relevant fact and attach it to the exact variant. “Selected size lists a 58-centimetre chest width” is neutral. “Perfect oversized fit” is interpretation and possibly preference. “Bundle lists bag and short strap” is a fact; “everything needed” assumes your use. Include the source type and checking date. If the statement comes from a creator caption, customer review or image rather than an option-linked specification, label that source and lower confidence where appropriate. Do not turn words such as premium, comfortable, durable or authentic into facts unless the evidence supports a precise claim, which a listing usually cannot do. Neutral wording may feel less exciting, but it gives the comparison a stable base.`],
      ["Translate each fact into the purchase brief", `Interpretation asks what the fact means for the named use, not whether it sounds positive. Compare the 58-centimetre garment width with a measured reference garment and the layering allowance you chose. Compare bag dimensions with the objects that must fit. Compare bundle contents with the component list required for the task. Show the calculation or rule. Another reader with a different body, device or use may reach another interpretation from the same fact, and that is acceptable. Keep unknowns explicit when the fact is incomplete. A listed outer dimension may not prove internal capacity; a material name may not predict performance. Interpretation should narrow the decision without claiming more than the evidence can carry.`],
      ["Give preference its own honest place", `Personal taste matters because an eligible item that you dislike may not be used. Record preference after the must-have interpretation: preferred colour, simpler silhouette, decorative detail, pocket arrangement or lower visual bulk. Use an ordered list or a small weight rather than calling taste a fact. Explain why the preference matters to likely use. “The simpler shape works with three existing outfits” is stronger than “looks better,” while still remaining personal. Do not use popularity, creator enthusiasm or high review volume as a substitute for your preference or as proof of performance. A scorecard becomes more trustworthy when it admits taste instead of hiding it inside supposedly objective categories such as value or quality.`],
      ["Prevent double counting", `Check whether one fact is influencing several rows. A single measurement should not earn points for fit, comfort and quality unless each interpretation has separate support. One set of photographs should not independently prove colour accuracy, construction and durability. Combine overlapping criteria or state the different evidence required. Double counting makes a preferred candidate look mathematically superior without adding information. Also separate information completeness from product performance. A page with clearer facts can receive stronger evidence confidence, but that does not automatically mean the product performs better. This audit keeps the scorecard small and shows where the result truly comes from. Four independent criteria often provide more insight than ten overlapping ones.`],
      ["Use confidence beside interpretation", `Add high, medium, low or absent confidence to each interpretation. High may require direct current evidence tied to the exact option. Medium may rely on relevant but general information. Low may come from an old or variant-unclear report. Absent means there is no usable basis. Do not turn confidence into a product-quality score. It describes the support behind your conclusion. When a high-weight interpretation has low confidence, pause or calculate a range. When only a low-weight preference is uncertain, decide whether you accept it. The label guides the next check and keeps a polished page from receiving unearned certainty. Record conflicts rather than choosing the version that favours your preferred candidate.`],
      ["Compare outcomes without claiming an objective best", `After gates, calculate or rank only the eligible candidates. Summarise each in the same pattern: strongest supported fit with the brief, largest unresolved uncertainty and leading personal preference. If one wins on use-case evidence and another wins on taste, state the trade-off. You can rationally choose the first for a high-consequence daily need and the second for a lower-consequence occasional use, provided both pass the requirements. There is no need to label either universally best. If totals are close, return to the original job, downside of error and confidence on the most important fact. The scorecard should clarify the choice, not produce a market-wide ranking from a personal brief.`],
      ["Save a conclusion that can be updated", `Write a final paragraph with the exact option, decisive listed facts, your interpretation, acknowledged preference and remaining unknown. Reopen the page before acting. If price, images, specification or option status changed, update the fact column and allow the interpretation to change. Do not protect the earlier winner from new evidence. The conclusion may become choose another candidate, request clarification or choose none. This revision path is the main advantage of separating layers: a changed fact does not require rewriting your taste, and a changed preference does not alter the historical evidence. The scorecard cannot guarantee quality, authenticity, delivery or satisfaction. It can show exactly which parts of the decision came from the page, which came from your use case and which came from you.`],
    ],
  },
} satisfies Record<PrioritySlug, FullArticle>;

